+
+## Historical scalar qualification (before native execution migration)
+
+The M9 working tree adds `src/vgi_scalar_duckdb2_profile.cpp`, a synchronous
+scalar path that reuses VGI's actual Arrow IPC and connection implementation.
+Catalog-owned scalar overloads need fixed concrete positional arguments and a
+single static result column. Overloads with ANY inputs, constant/named/variadic
+arguments, defaults, TABLE input or dynamic output types are not registered in
+this profile. Other supported overloads in the catalog remain usable.
+
+Native binding constructs Arrow metadata and captures settings without invoking
+the worker. The first nonempty execution batch acquires the current original
+catalog transaction, binds and initializes a real worker connection, then
+exchanges one Arrow batch per DuckDB input batch. One output row per input row
+is mandatory. The profile always validates output types and Arrow buffers,
+including when general optional worker validation is disabled. It performs no
+input deduplication, result memoization or scalar-specific optimizer rewrites.
+Worker errors are marked fallible in DuckDB; failed connections are destroyed
+and never returned to the worker pool. Successful connections retain the
+existing bounded POSIX I/O and pooling behavior.
+
+The following scalar qualification describes the earlier M9 implementation; it
+is not a new units-worker qualification against the current GitHub host.
+
+Cardinality validation covers each returned execution batch. Successful scalar
+state cleanup closes the input stream and drains EOS before pooling; an extra
+post-input batch discards the connection, but a native local-state destructor
+cannot report a new query error. This is therefore qualification of the selected
+units worker's one-batch-per-process contract, not full adversarial stream
+finalization for arbitrary providers.
+
+Direct native SQL can constant-fold a consistent scalar. The historical
+Crossfield private scalar execution wrapper has since been removed. Current
+Crossfield queries use DuckDB native provider callbacks and normal scheduling;
+these earlier scalar results do not requalify the current units integration.
+
+`test/duckdb2/verify_scalar_units.py` exercises the real Rust units worker through
+the matching native DuckDB C API: linear and offset conversions, temperature
+differences, negative values, strict unknown/missing/incompatible unit errors,
+valid NULLs, nonfinite/overflow rejection, repeated 10,001-row prepared results,
+late-batch failure/recovery and cancellation/recovery with both optimizer modes.
+It records artifact hashes and a canonical sorted unit-catalog digest. The
+worker must implement protocol 2.1.0; the original units SDK 0.32 speaks 1.5.0
+and is rejected. The compatible worker uses VGI 0.43.0, RPC 0.31.1 and Arrow 60
+under its lockfile, with strict function contract 1 and implementation 0.1.6.
+
+Run it with `--library`, `--vgi`, `--worker`, `--crossfield` (the sibling project
+directory supplying the C API test helper) and `--output`. The separate governed
+runner is `crossfield/scripts/verify_scalar_vgi.py`.
+
+`test/duckdb2/verify_scalar_transport.py` adds an actual byte-stall proxy around
+the Rust worker. It proves interruption and subsequent recovery while a scalar
+RPC is blocked; `--with-timeout` also exercises the real 30-second idle ceiling.
+This isolation disables the ordinary optimizer: constant propagation from
+single-row table statistics can otherwise attempt a scalar fold, swallow its
+fallible error, then retry at execution, consuming two separate idle bounds.
+Neither transport test
+asserts a hard wall-clock bound for a complete semantic query.
+
+## Current GitHub worker qualification
+
+The working-tree adapter also builds against DuckDB
+`94abd4ed366569345faf5dc1e254ae6ca7311a76` with Crossfield's ordered core patches.
+It uses the engine's external `BindInfo` scan classification and ordinary scalar
+registration; the retired scalar callback-pointer identity setter is removed.
+Crossfield's fifth generic patch preserves named scalar options on correlated
+table in-out calls. Without it, a call such as `pulls(r.repo, max_rows => 0)`
+incorrectly treated the keyword value as a second positional input column.
+
+`test/duckdb2/github_catalog.sql` binds all 15 GitHub table functions without API
+reads, including named defaults and LIST result columns.
+`test/duckdb2/github_values.sql` checks independently chosen numerical answers
+through the real GitHub worker and its native callbacks. It expects the
+`vgi-github/tests/crossfield/worker_fixture.py` subprocess worker attached as
+`github`, with `reporting true` and `auth 'off'`. The fixture replaces only HTTP
+transport; it retains Arrow IPC, worker binding, paging and strict validation.
+Python is the provider implementation, not the test engine.
+
+The matching EC2 module is built with `VGI_ENABLE_IROH=OFF`,
+`VGI_DUCKDB2_INVOCATION_PROFILE=ON`, `GIT_COMMIT_HASH` set to the above SHA, and
+`DUCKDB_EXPLICIT_VERSION=v2.0.0-dev0`. Set the source hash when reusing an older
+build directory so the extension footer matches the actual host. The GitHub
+worker's locked dependencies are VGI Python 0.40.0 and VGI RPC 0.48.0.
+This qualification uses native subprocess transport; HTTP/Iroh transport and
+the `rate_limit` catalog table remain outside the isolated profile. Its existing
+`all_rate_limit()` function provides the same data through a native producer.
+
+## Reproducing the current GitHub profile
+
+The companion patches in `vgi-github/tests/crossfield/` reproduce this profile:
+`native-vgi-profile-base.patch` exports local commit
+`1599d86632e092ad4a53497d49960dc10aff1fc3` against upstream
+`de68c1786d517ad65bce535bc00d3c2e54d18c1e`; `native-vgi-profile.patch` adds the
+previously uncommitted fixed-schema scalar adapter and current GitHub changes:
+exact keyword defaults, external scan metadata, and removal of the retired
+scalar callback identity API. Starting at the upstream commit, apply the base
+patch and then the current patch with `git apply`. Starting at the isolated
+profile commit, apply only the current patch. No build artifacts or historical
+Python test harnesses are included. VGI retains its own license and remains a
+separately built extension, not a Crossfield source dependency.
+
+Point a DuckDB extension configuration at the patched VGI directory, for example:
+
+```cmake
+duckdb_extension_load(vgi SOURCE_DIR /path/to/vgi-profile)
+```
+
+Configure a separate build directory against the same patched DuckDB source as
+Crossfield (including `0005-table-in-out-keyword-arguments.patch`):
+
+```sh
+cmake -S /path/to/duckdb -B /path/to/build-vgi \
+  -DDUCKDB_EXTENSION_CONFIGS=/path/to/vgi-profile-config.cmake \
+  -DVGI_ENABLE_IROH=OFF -DVGI_DUCKDB2_INVOCATION_PROFILE=ON \
+  -DGIT_COMMIT_HASH=94abd4ed366569345faf5dc1e254ae6ca7311a76 \
+  -DDUCKDB_EXPLICIT_VERSION=v2.0.0-dev0 \
+  -DArrow_DIR=/path/to/arrow/share/arrow
+cmake --build /path/to/build-vgi --target vgi_loadable_extension -j20
+```
+
+The EC2 Arrow package used for this build is at
+`/home/ec2-user/Development/vgi/build/release/vcpkg_installed/arm64-linux/share/arrow`.
+Use the resulting module at `extension/vgi/vgi.duckdb_extension` with the matching
+DuckDB host. The worker checkout uses `uv sync --locked`; no DuckDB Python package
+is used. The model example's native SQLLogicTest is the current integration
+acceptance test; the earlier scalar Python harnesses are historical evidence.
